Excellent questions, Sai. Here are my evidence-based answers drawn directly from the workbook analysis I just performed:

1. Procedure Quality — Derivation of 108 Procedures

The 108 procedures were derived by mapping each audit's specific objective and scope text to conceptually relevant analytics techniques. Each audit received 2–4 procedures (mean: 3.1), with the count varying based on scope breadth — audits with richer scope detail (e.g., Audit 18 – Reinsurance, Audit 20 – Complaints, Audit 33 – T&E) received 4 procedures, while those with limited scope (Audits 39, 40) received only 2.

Three anonymised examples of the mapping logic:

- Pricing audit example: The source objective references "pricing controls and capabilities" with scope covering "Data; Assumptions; Modelling; Optimisation; Validation; Deployment; Monitoring." This was mapped to three procedures: model output profiling (→ Modelling), technical vs. actual premium variance analysis (→ Validation/Monitoring), and rating factor outlier detection (→ Data/Optimisation). Each procedure traces to a specific scope element.

- Complaint management example: The source scope explicitly covers "root cause analysis", "trend analysis", and "regulatory compliance." These were directly mapped to: root cause categorisation analysis, complaint volume trending, regulatory response timeliness testing, and reoccurrence pattern analysis. The fourth procedure was added because the objective specifically mentions "action taken to prevent reoccurrence."

- IT security example: The source scope covers "identify, protect, detect, respond and recover from insider threat events." This was mapped to DLP alert log analysis (→ detect), insider threat event pattern analysis (→ identify/respond), and recommendation implementation tracking (→ linked to the explicit "follow up on prior IA audit recommendations" in the objective).

However, there is a significant honesty point: while procedures were themed to audit objectives, some procedure types were reused across similar audits with identical naming — this is addressed in Question 6.

