The lower match scores arise because the "Related" text uses paraphrased or abbreviated scope references rather than verbatim source text. The connection is conceptually reasonable but not verbatim-traceable in these cases. This is a quality gap — ideally, the Related field should quote the source scope directly.

3. Data Requirements — Specificity and Availability

Each of the 108 procedures includes a "Potential Data Requirements" field identifying conceptual data types (e.g., "Pricing model output data; Assumption parameters", "Complaint register data"). These are generic data categories, not specific field names, table names, or system references.

Critically, no data availability has been confirmed. Every single procedure (108/108) includes a Limitations/Caveats entry:

- 82 procedures state data formats or systems are "unknown"

- 18 procedures state data availability is "not confirmed"

- 8 procedures use other caveat language (e.g., "requires access to both technical and transactional pricing data")

- Zero procedures have a blank Limitation field

Honest assessment: The data requirements are exploratory placeholders. They identify what types of data would conceptually be needed, but do not reference specific company systems, databases, extracts, or confirmed availability. No distinction is made between "data exists but access is uncertain" versus "data may not exist at all."

4. Audits with Partial Information (IDs 39 and 40)

What was available in the source:

- Audit ID, Audit Name, Legal Entity (AZI, LVIC, HICO), Planned Quarter (Q4), Audit Category (Local)

- A high-level objective: "To complete the mandatory Entity Level Control Assessment / Non-Financial Risk Management testing covering the in-scope controls for Internal Audit testing in 2024"

What was missing:

- The specific in-scope controls are defined by Group and were not listed in the source PowerPoint

- No detailed scope breakdown of individual control areas, processes, or risk categories

- The Audit Objective and Audit Scope fields in Sheet 2 contain identical text because the source did not separate them

Impact on exploratory suggestions:

- Both audits received only 2 procedures (the minimum), compared to 3–4 for other audits

- The procedures are very generic: "Control testing sample completeness analysis" and "Testing coverage profiling"

- The procedures for both audits are nearly identical in structure and language — differing only by "ELCA" vs. "NFRM"

- Without the Group-defined control lists, it was impossible to suggest targeted analytics procedures

- The workbook correctly flags both as "Partial" extraction status on Sheet 4

5. Completeness Validation — 100% vs. 33+2

These measure different things:

- 100% extraction completeness (35/35): Every audit record identified in the source Annual Audit Plan was successfully extracted into the workbook. No audit was omitted. This is a record-level completeness measure.

- 33 complete + 2 partial: This measures field-level completeness — whether all 10 extraction fields for each audit were fully populated with meaningful, distinct content. Audits 39 and 40 are marked "Partial" because their Objective and Scope fields contain identical text (no separate scope detail was available) and their in-scope controls are undefined in the source.

Both measures are valid but answer different questions. The Executive Summary reports 100% extraction completeness (all 35 records present), while Sheet 4 provides the more granular field-level assessment showing 33 complete and 2 partial.

